{"name":"Army Green","hex":"#4b5320","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"army green","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several hues of dull green used by various militaries for their uniforms."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/army_green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Army Green football team","blocks":[{"text":"Army Green is one of the football sections that represents the Indian Army. The team regularly participates in the Durand Cup and various regional tournaments. The team's major achievement was in 2016 Durand Cup as they won the title by beating NEROCA FC in the final."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/fe/Army_Green_%28football_team%29.svg/330px-Army_Green_%28football_team%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Army_Green_football_team"},{"title":"Olive (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Olive, also called olive green, is a dark shade of yellowish green, like that of unripe or green olives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg/330px-Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_(color)"},{"title":"Army","blocks":[{"text":"An army, ground force or land force is an armed force that fights primarily on land. In the broadest sense, it is the land-based military branch, service branch, or armed service of a nation or country. It may also include aviation assets by possessing an army aviation component. Within a national military force, the word army may also mean a field army."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/250th_United_States_Army_Birthday_Parade_in_Washington%2C_D.C._on_June_14%2C_2025_-_262.jpg/330px-250th_United_States_Army_Birthday_Parade_in_Washington%2C_D.C._on_June_14%2C_2025_-_262.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Army"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/SR_Maunsell_saloon_%289132055782%29.jpg/500px-SR_Maunsell_saloon_%289132055782%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/SR_Maunsell_saloon_%289132055782%29.jpg/500px-SR_Maunsell_saloon_%289132055782%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASR_Maunsell_saloon_(9132055782).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/178-%D0%B9_%D0%BE%D0%BC%D1%81%D0%B1_6-%D0%B9_%D0%B3%D0%B2.%D0%BE%D0%BC%D1%81%D0%B1%D1